-- title:
--   Components of the special fibre of X₁(Mp) as Cartier divisors
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, a nonzero $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, a characteristic-zero field $L$ which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$, and an $L$-subfield $K$ of the Laurent series field over $L$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$, with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$, and let $j \in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion the image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157). Let $k$ be an algebraically closed $A$-algebra field of characteristic $p$, and write $X_k$ for the fibre product of [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) (the two-chart model, the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $A$-subalgebras of $K$ generated by $j$ and by $j^{-1}$) with $\operatorname{Spec} k$. Let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, equipped with closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ into $X_k$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ whose images cover $X_k$, such that the scheme-theoretic intersection $C_1 \times_{X_k} C_2$ is reduced with exactly $n > 0$ points. Let $O$ be a discrete valuation domain with a ring map $\rho_O : A \to O$ carrying the maximal ideal of $A$ onto that of $O$, together with $O \to k$ compatible with $A \to k$, and let $bc : X_k \to X_O$ be a morphism to the corresponding fibre product over $\operatorname{Spec} O$ commuting with the projection to the model and inducing $\operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} O$ on the base. Let $z : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to (\operatorname{Spec} k \to C_1 \times_{X_k} C_2)$ be $n$ points whose projections are sections of $c_1$ and of $c_2$ and which are pairwise distinct on closed points. Writing $I_1$ and $I_2$ for the ideal sheaf data given by the kernels of $i_1$ followed by $bc$, and of $i_2$ followed by $bc$, the conclusion asserts: the modules $I_1$ and $I_2$ (the kernels of the unit sheaf mapping to the pushforward of the unit sheaf along the respective closed subscheme inclusions) are invertible, in the sense that every point of $X_O$ has an open neighbourhood over which the restricted module is isomorphic to the unit module; the tensor product of the duals of $I_1$ and $I_2$ is isomorphic to the unit module on $X_O$; the pullback of the dual of $I_1$ along $C_1 \times_{\operatorname{Spec} k} \operatorname{Spec} k \to C_1 \to X_k \to X_O$ is isomorphic to the $n$-fold tensor product, formed by right-folding over `List.finRange n`, of the modules of the degree-one relative effective Cartier divisors `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` attached to the sections $z_i$ of $c_1$, namely the kernel ideal sheaves of their graphs; and the pullback of the dual of $I_1$ along the analogous morphism through $C_2$ is isomorphic to the corresponding $n$-fold tensor product of the duals of the point divisor modules for the sections $z_i$ of $c_2$.
--
--   This is the intersection-theoretic input on the regular model of $X_1(Mp)$ after unramified base change: each component of the geometric special fibre is a Cartier divisor, the two components add up to the divisor of the uniformiser, and the restrictions of $\mathcal{O}(-C_1)^{\vee}$ to $C_1$ and $C_2$ are $\mathcal{O}(-\sum z_i)$ and $\mathcal{O}(\sum z_i)$, which encodes $C_1 \cdot C_2 = n$ and $C_1^2 = -n$. It feeds the subsequent Euler-characteristic computations for tensor powers of these modules on the components.
